      "Language": "jpn",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 77-year-old woman presented with a high fever. She had a history of resection of the extrahepatic bile duct, cholecystectomy, and hepaticojejunostomy for a congenital choledochal cyst, 23 years previously. Computed tomography showed a tumor measuring 90mm behind the head of the pancreas. This tumor appeared to invade the duodenum and pancreas, although swollen lymph nodes and distant metastasis were not detected. The patient was diagnosed with a carcinoma arising from the intrapancreatic remnant bile duct. A subtotal stomach-preserving pancreaticoduodenectomy and regional lymphadenectomy were performed. The patient remains alive and well with no evidence of disease 11 months after resection."},
